cast:
  Zofia:
  Morwenna:
  Ollie:
  Grace:
  Ada:
  Hale:
~~~~
missing_author
---
Zofia: The caption. It says you translated Menabrea's article and his Notes. Is that actually wrong, or just annoying?
Ada: If the Notes are assigned to him, the sum contains another person's work under the wrong name. Annoyance is not the error I ask you to correct. [class=dead]
Zofia: She says the Notes weren't his. That's the error.
Morwenna: I have the publication details. In 1843 she translated Luigi Menabrea's article about the Analytical Engine and added her own Notes A to G. About three times as long as the original article. If I've printed the whole file. Yes, here.
Ada: I had supposed those labels sufficient. Evidently the operation fails in transmission. Read me the date upon your copy, please. [class=dead]
Zofia: Downloaded today. Not something you missed while you were alive. I'll put today beside when we showed you.
Morwenna: And the caption gets its own page. It mustn't look as if it came from the plaque.
Zofia: You worked with Charles Babbage on the Analytical Engine. Was it basically a giant calculator?
Ada: If you restrict the operations to quantities, you restrict the result before the work begins. Symbols may stand for more than numbers; music, for instance, offers relations upon which operations might act. [class=dead]
Zofia: She's objecting to “calculator”. Numbers aren't the only things it could work with. She suggested music too.
Morwenna: A proposed mechanical general-purpose computer. And Note G gives a method for calculating Bernoulli numbers on it. Often called the first published computer program. I underlined “published” because losing that word changes the claim.
Zofia: Bernoulli numbers can stay in their own petition. We need help with instructions that people won't wriggle out of.
Ada: Then bring me the instructions, rather than an assurance that they are good ones. A faulty operation does not improve through confidence. [class=dead]
Morwenna: Life card first? Augusta Ada Byron, later Countess of Lovelace, 1815 to 1852. Father, Lord Byron.
Ada: If my father's name occupies the first line and my work the last, your arrangement requires a second calculation. [class=dead]
Zofia: Notes first. She has spotted our layout.
Morwenna: We have the room booked. Only until the return time. Will she come?
Zofia: Ada, will you come with me? You can't go ahead. I'm the transport, apparently.
Ada: An irregular conveyance; nevertheless, if it reaches the table, we may begin. [class=dead]

forty_days
---
Hale: If the Charter forbids delay of justice, mistress, let your request require redress. A supplication which grants its judge leave to do nothing is ill drawn. [class=dead]
Zofia: He wants “require”, again. Because Magna Carta says they can't delay justice.
Ollie: Could write it bigger. People obey capital letters. Well, the swimming pool thinks so.
Morwenna: They also have someone watching the pool. The Charter had enforcement terms. We need to read those before we borrow the promise.
Hale: Read them entire, madam. If the Crown was bound, I would know by what bond; not by a schoolroom abbreviation. [class=dead]
Zofia: He says read the enforcement bit. Not the nice shortened version.
Morwenna: King John sealed the draft charter on 15 June 1215 at Runnymede, between Windsor and Staines, beside the Thames. It followed the barons' revolt over his heavy financial demands and the failed French campaign of 1214. Robert fitz Walter led the rebels.
Zofia: So it wasn't a king waking up and deciding to be fair.
Morwenna: No. Look here: twenty-five barons to monitor compliance. If he failed to comply within forty days, they could seize his castles and lands.
Hale: Then the bond had teeth, mistress. Put teeth in your demand, lest it be received with a bow and left beneath the inkpot. [class=dead]
Zofia: He likes the castles bit. We haven't got any barons.
Grace: We haven't even got everyone to return a pen.
Morwenna: Clause 39 protects a free man against arrest, imprisonment and other penalties except by lawful judgment of his peers or the law of the land. Clause 40 promises not to sell, deny or delay right or justice to anyone. Those are different clauses. Neither page says a dead witness can order us to reverse his case.
Hale: If “anyone” bears its ordinary force, madam, by what warrant do you diminish it? I was a subject, not an entry mislaid by a clerk. [class=dead]
Zofia: He says “anyone” ought to include him. He was a person.
Morwenna: He was. That doesn't make this extract the guarantee he wants. And the 1215 charter didn't just stay in force untouched. Pope Innocent III annulled it that August.
Zofia: Wait. Already?
Morwenna: Already. Reissued in 1216 and 1217 under the regency for the young Henry III, then in the definitive version on 11 February 1225. Edward I confirmed it as statute law in 1297. We can't skip all that and call this one unbroken instruction.
Hale: If the instrument was annulled and made anew, the later words must be examined. I concede the sequence; I do not concede that your present arrangement does justice. [class=dead]
Zofia: He's accepting the dates. He's definitely not accepting the draft.

first_for_whom
---
Zofia: So Poland had habeas corpus first? The Jedlnia privilege, 1430. Neminem captivabimus. No imprisonment without a court judgment.
Morwenna: First compared with which part of that pile? We have just read 1215. And who had the protection in your example?
Zofia: Nobles. Fine. That wasn't everyone either. I was trying to get him off Runnymede, not start a competition.
Hale: If a privilege names an estate, mistress, the estate is part of its meaning. Strike it out, and you enlarge the grant without the grantor. [class=dead]
Zofia: He's enjoying agreeing with you. I can tell.
Morwenna: Magna Carta put royal power within rules instead of the king's personal will. It protected the Church and limited feudal payments. Those are changes. We don't have to make them universal for them to matter.
Zofia: This sentence, then. “The ancient guarantees protect all people always.” Gone.
Morwenna: Please cross it out properly. You leave half a word and somebody will type it back in.
Zofia: Who remains excluded from our draft? Dead people, unless a living person brings the evidence. People who can't write it themselves. Anyone whose publisher won't answer.
Hale: If you bring my reason in your own hand, mistress, do not put your belief where my evidence should stand. The distinction shall bind you also. [class=dead]
Zofia: He wants me held to it too. Yes. I heard that part.

six_pictures
---
Zofia: Six pictures. Six surviving originals. That should stop him saying we've only looked at somebody's summary.
Grace: Why do two of your originals have the same catalogue label?
Zofia: They don't. One has a corner folded—oh. The photograph has a corner folded. It's the same sheet.
Ada: If you copy a quantity onto a second page, you have multiplied its appearances, not its value. Your count requires distinct objects. [class=dead]
Zofia: Ada says the same thing, with maths. I'll delete the extra images.
Grace: Keep them. Mark them as duplicates. Otherwise tomorrow you find them again and we're back to six.
Zofia: Four surviving exemplifications of the 1215 charter. Two at the British Library, one at Lincoln Castle, one at Salisbury Cathedral. There. Four objects, six pictures.
Grace: But why do we still do this? Would Will want us counting old sheets, or getting his complaint read? Have you asked him?
Morwenna: He might want it sent now. I still won't send six copies as six sources. It would give them a reason to dismiss him.
Grace: Then checking it has to end somewhere. We need a person, an appointment and a finishing time.
Ada: If the checking has no stopping condition, your procedure can consume the entire afternoon without producing a request. Set the condition before proceeding. [class=dead]
Zofia: One last look, then we send. Even if Hale still hates a word. Grace, you can sign before your bus.

request_we_can_sign
---
Morwenna: Attachment corrected. Grace needs the finished version before we use her name.
Zofia: Title: Request for answers about disputed historical accounts. Then “We require—”
Ollie: You crossed that out.
Zofia: It sneaked back. “We ask for an investigation and a route for evidence-backed corrections, with reasons when a correction is refused.”
Ada: If a refusal is permitted, specify that it must address the demand. An answer to a neighbouring question may be perfectly exact and perfectly useless. [class=dead]
Zofia: Add “to the particular statement or omission raised”. She caught a hole.
Hale: If refusal counts as an answer, mistress, you may deliver my petition and return with the injury dressed in a fresh heading. I shall not call that satisfaction. [class=dead]
Zofia: Hale says a refusal might just put a new heading on the same injury. I know. I still can't promise the verdict.
Ollie: Are we asking all of Parliament? That needs a bigger envelope. Probably.
Morwenna: The Commons for this request. Parliament has three components: the Sovereign, or Crown, the House of Lords and the House of Commons. A member helping us doesn't mean all three have agreed anything.
Zofia: So the signatures don't bind them.
Morwenna: No. For the paper-petition route we're using, we need a living member to present it. We collect living signatures. Not marks copied for ghosts.
Zofia: I'll sign first. Not for Hale. For me asking them to look.
Hale: Then sign, mistress, provided my reason remains attached. I withhold my approval of the remedy; I shall not prevent you putting the question. [class=dead]
Zofia: He'll let me finish reading. That might be his signature equivalent. It isn't going on the sheet.
Ollie: Where's the Workshop answer?
Zofia: Same receipt. No answer about Will. No answer about Doyle. Nothing about the animals.
Morwenna: Then ask for the appointment. We need someone who can make ignoring us visible.
Ada: Before you dispatch it, read the class of persons admitted by your first line. A precise exclusion remains an exclusion. [class=dead]
Zofia: “People unable to speak for themselves.” Right. The horse isn't a person. None of the animals gets through this sentence.
Ollie: Can we leave a space? I don't want to be the one who crosses out the horse.
Zofia: I'm leaving that line open. We need someone to hear why.

one_appointment
---
Morwenna: Mara Reed's office replied. Tomorrow. One appointment. One case slot; they want the papers tonight.
Zofia: Ours. Send ours.
Ollie: Niamh asked us about her mum's care record before we had this draft. Grace will ask what happened to that.
Zofia: We could put both in the folder.
Morwenna: It says one. I asked whether that meant one folder. They said one case.
Ada: If the capacity is one and the claims are two, a larger envelope will not alter the capacity. [class=dead]
Zofia: I heard you. I just wanted it to.
Ollie: Call Niamh before you send anything.
